\chapter{Cosmic rays and extensive air showers}
\label{chap:cosmic-rays}

\CRs are particles of extraterrestrial origin that travel very close to the 
speed of light. Their relativistic kinetic energy, $pc$, far surpasses their 
rest mass $mc^2$. In particular, \UHECRs are typically defined as \CRs with 
energies exceeding $\SI{1}{\exa\eV} = \SI{e18}{\eV}$ 
\cite{alvesbatistaOpenQuestionsCR2019}. \EHECRs($>\SI{100}{\exa\eV}$), being
microscopic particles themselves, carry macroscopic amounts of energy, and must
originate from the most energetic processes in the universe. 

Naturally, \CRs are of great interest to researchers. Not only do they hold the
key to understanding particle interactions at extremely high energies and small
scales, but can also improve the understanding of the cosmos and its' evolution
over the largest distances and longest timescales.

\cref{sec:cr-phenomenology} gives an evidence based introduction to the
energetic messengers. The subsequent sections follow in spirit the voyage of
\CRs. First, their creation at a source accelerator is discussed in 
\cref{sec:cr-accelerators}. Next the \CR transport through the universe is 
highlighted in \cref{sec:cr-propagation}. Finally, the violent end of the 
elementary particles in the upper atmosphere of Earth is analyzed in 
\cref{sec:extensive-air-showers}. 

\newpage
\section{Cosmic Ray Phenomenology}
\label{sec:cr-phenomenology}

\halflift

\subsection{Historical Discovery}
\label{ssec:cr-history}

The study of cosmic rays is an active area of research and a field more than 
one hundred years in the making. The turn of the twentieth century saw many 
physicists testing the predictions of the newly developed theory of 
electromagnetism. Of special importance for this story are the advancements of 
Theodor Wulf, who, in 1909, developed an electrometer that is able to measure 
the ionization rate of the atmosphere. His invention was used to show the 
difference in air ionization rates on the ground, at the top of the Eiffel 
Tower \cite{wulfBeobachtungenUberStrahlung1910} and underwater
\cite{paciniRadiazionePenetranteAlla1912}. 

A general consensus was not reached, but all evidence seemed to suggest an 
altitude dependence of electrometer ionization rates. In 1912 Victor Hess 
continued this study by examining spontaneous ionization during ballon flights 
above Germany and Austria \cite{hessElektrischeLeitfaehigkeitAtmosphaere1926}. 
With the ionization rate increasing the higher the balloon flew, Hess concluded
that energetic particles are present in the atmosphere, and that their density 
profile increases at altitude. His intuition proved correct and would 
eventually be honored with a Nobel prize in 1936 
\cite{nobelprizeoutreach2025NobelPrizePhysicsa}.

Given the context of research at the time, these energetic particles were
thought to be \EM radiation. Their extraterrestrial origin was demonstrated by 
Hess himself, as well as Werner Kolhörster in 1913/1914 
\cite{kolhorsterMeasurementsPenetratingRadiation1913}. Consequentially, the 
term \textit{cosmic rays} was coined for the phenomenon. 

In 1929 Walter Bothe and Kolhörster attempted to investigate the nature of 
cosmic rays further by studying their interactions with high-Z materials, 
assuming that the proposed \EM-nature of the cosmic rays would not allow them 
to traverse deeply into the absorbers \cite{botheNaturHohenstrahlung1929}. They
were surprised to find out that the particles they investigated had a 
penetrating power uncharacteristic for $\upgamma$-radiation. They concluded 
that \CRs are therefore made up of, at least partially, corpuscular radiation, 
rather than photons. This started a subsequent investigation of the type and 
nature of \CRs and was an important milestone for the field of astroparticle 
physics.

Since then, the study of \CRs has lead to the discovery of the positron 
(Anderson 1933, Ref.\ \cite{andersonPositiveElectron1933}), the muon (Anderson,
Neddermeyer 1936, Ref.\ \cite{andersonElementaryParticlesMatter1949}), and a 
plethora of other particles. Today we know that \CRs are very diverse in 
nature. Their composition ranges from elementary particles like electrons, over
exotic mesons, possibly to heavy nuclei. Photons too have been detected in \CR 
experiments, proving that the original name is not a complete misnomer.

Another important milestone was further achieved by Pierre Auger in 1938. He 
conducted coincidence measurements in the swiss Alps and found that the rate of
coincidences between several \CR detectors is directly proportional to their 
separation \cite{augerExtensiveCosmicRayShowers1939}. This marks the discovery 
of \EAS, and the beginning of ground based \CR research in array detectors.

\subsection{Cosmic Ray Observables}

The many discoveries of new fragments in the cosmic radiation has resulted in
the creation of many new theories that aim to describe the diverse particle zoo
at hand. 

In general though, already a handful parameters, called hereafter \CR 
observables, are sufficient to characterize the kinematics of a cosmic ray from
genesis at the source, over propagation, to detection on Earth. It follows a 
brief introduction of the most important properties defining a \CR.

\subsubsection{Energy}
\label{sssec:energy}

The energy of the \CR particle is perhaps its' most defining observable. After 
a particle leaves a \CR acceleration region (\cref{sec:cr-accelerators}) it 
begins a journey mostly consisting of adiabatic processes, keeping its initial
energy almost constant. This means the \CR energy directly dictates how (and 
how far) the particle propagates through space. Moreover it also determines
several aspects of the atmospheric particle cascade resulting from the 
destruction on Earth (Ref.\ \cref{sec:extensive-air-showers}).

\cref{eq:snr-spectral-index} will reveal that the energy distribution, also 
called the energy spectrum, of most \CR accelerators is a power law 
$\Phi(E) \propto E^{-\gamma}$ with a single spectral index $\gamma$.
\footnote{And eventual cutoff at a maximum attainable energy, see e.g.\ 
\cref{sssec:snr-injection-and-escape}} The energy spectrum observed on Earth,
as shown in \cref{fig:cr-spectrum}, does not follow this functionally simple 
form. Instead, it shows several different kinks, corresponding to a hardening 
(softening) of the spectrum, where the average energy of a \CR increases 
(decreases) above the kink energy. These features in general correspond to 
transition between different \CR populations, that each have a respective 
particle flux, injection and cutoff energy. To date, several prominent features
have been identified in the \CR spectrum observed on Earth:

\begin{itemize}
	\vspace{0.7cm}
	\item \textbf{The knee} refers to a softening of the spectrum around 
	\SI{4}{\peta\eV}. The spectral index changes from $\gamma\approx2.7$
	below the knee to $\gamma\approx3.1$ above the knee 
	\cite{blumerCosmicRaysKnee2009}. It has been shown that this coincides
	with a transition in the mean mass of \CR particles 
	\cite{lhaasocollaborationMeasurementsAllParticleEnergy2024}. The knee 
	is thus thought to mark the transition from light \CRs 
	($m_\mathrm{CR} \leq m_\mathrm{He}$) to heavier hadronic cosmic rays.

	\vspace{0.7cm}
	\item \textbf{The second knee} is a further softening from 
	$\gamma\approx3.1$ at an energy below roughly \SI{500}{\peta\eV} to 
	$\gamma\approx3.25$ above \cite{naganoEnergySpectrumPrimary1992, 
	lawrenceCosmicRayEnergy1991, birdEvidenceCorrelatedChanges1993, 
	abu-zayyadMeasurementCosmicRayEnergy2001}. Like the softening that 
	occurs at \SI{4}{\peta\eV}, this feature is assumed to be composition
	related. However, mass sensitive studies around the second knee to date
	have not been able to confirm this theory with sufficient certainty.

	\vspace{0.7cm}
	\item \textbf{The ankle} marks the transition from $\gamma\approx3.25$ 
	to $\gamma\approx2.5$ at an energy of roughly \SI{5}{\exa\eV} 
	\cite{fenuCosmicRayEnergy2023}. The Pierre Auger observatory has 
	reported on a dipole in the arrival directions of \CRs above 
	\SI{8}{\exa\eV} (Ref.\ \cref{fig:dipole} and 
	\cite{thepierreaugercollaborationObservationLargescaleAnisotropy2017}), 
	coincident with the hardening of the energy spectrum. Since the 
	anisotropy points away from the galactic center the ankle is projected
	to represent the transition from a predominantly galactic \CR 
	population to an extragalactic \CR population.

	\vspace{0.7cm}
	\item \textbf{The instep} is a softening of the \UHECR spectrum at 
	\SI{13}{\exa\eV} where $\gamma$ decreases from $\sim2.5$ to $\sim3.0$
	\cite{aabMeasurementCosmicrayEnergy2020}. This feature can be 
	reproduced in simulations when altering the \CR mass composition from 
	extragalactic light \CRs to extragalactic heavy \CRs 
	\cite{tsunesadaJointAnalysisEnergy2022}. Similar to the second knee 
	feature, experiments to date do not have a sufficiently good mass 
	sensitivity to confirm this theory.

	\vspace{0.7cm}
	\item \textbf{The suppression} marks the steep falloff at the tail-end 
	of the (measurable) \CR energy spectrum. At an energy above 
	$\sim\SI{50}{\exa\eV}$ the spectral index drastically changes to 
	$\gamma\approx5.4$ \cite{aabMeasurementCosmicrayEnergy2020}. Cosmic 
	rays exceeding this energy are incredibly sparse. It is theorized that
	no process exists in the local cosmos that can reliably accelerate 
	particles beyond this energy \cite{tsunesadaJointAnalysisEnergy2022, 
	greisenEndCosmicRaySpectrum1966, 
	kuzminCosmicrayInteractionsPhotons1968}. See \cref{sssec:gzk-cutoff}.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/spectrum.png}
  \caption{The distribution of energies of \CRs arriving on earth. Several 
	  transition regions are marked and explained in the main text. Data 
	  stem from \cite{re3data.orgKASCADECosmicRay2013} and 
  	  \cite{yushkovMassCompositionCosmic2021}.}
  \label{fig:cr-spectrum}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Mass}

An almost negligible fraction of the total energy is (for massive particles) 
given by the rest mass of the cosmic ray. Given the miniscule relative strength
of gravity,\footnote{Compared to the \EM-, strong or weak force.} mass terms 
can be safely ignored in \CR acceleration and propagation. However, for 
composite particles the energy per nucleon, $E_0/A$, which is a mass-dependent 
variable, heavily influences the development of particle cascades stemming from
inelastic interactions of the \CR with a target material (Ref.\ 
\cref{sssec:hadronic-primaries}).

\cref{fig:cr-composition} shows the distribution of different \CR primaries 
with energies up to a few \unit{\giga\eV} and sets them into context with the 
elemental abundance in the solar system. Both distributions largely agree, with
an exception around the atomic number $Z=4$ (Beryllium) and $Z=23$ (Vanadium). 
They are not common elements in the solar system due to radioactive 
instability. Meanwhile, the \CR mass composition shows an excess, which can be
explained by spallation of primary \CRs \cite{hillierGammaRayAstronomy1984}.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/cr_composition.png}
  \caption{The abundance of different elements relative to carbon ($=10^6$) as 
  measured in the solar system (black) and in \CRs with energies up to several 
  \unit{\giga\eV} (red), which appear to be more level. The data stem from 
  \cite{hillierGammaRayAstronomy1984}.}
  \label{fig:cr-composition}
\end{figure}

Moving to much higher energies, a direct detection of \CRs (via e.g.\ 
sattelite or balloon experiments) becomes unfeasible due to the miniscule 
particle flux. Ground-based \CR observatories which a much larger detector area
take over. Relying on indirect detection methods, these observatories do not 
have the same mass sensitivity as direct detectors, and can typically only 
separate a light from a heavy component 
\cite{pereiramartinsSearchMassDependent2024}. \UHECR analysis is usually 
grouped into four different mass proxies due to this inexactness. The chosen 
proxies are hydrogen ($Z=1$), helium ($Z=2$), iron ($Z=26$), and one (or all) 
of carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen ($Z\in[13,\,15]$).\footnote{The CNO-cycle leads
to a roughly equal abundance of all three elements, see 
\cref{fig:cr-composition}.} The relative fraction of these proxies evolves over
the \CR energy, as shown from measurements by the Pierre Auger Collaboration in
\cref{fig:auger-mix}. Hadronic interaction models suggest that light elements 
dominate the composition at low energies, whereas an increase in the mean 
logarithmic mass, $\langle\mathrm{ln}\,A\rangle$, is observed with an increase 
in the primary energy of the \CR particle 
\cite{mayotteMeasurementInterpretationUHECR2025}.

\begin{figure}[t]
