\subsubsection{Charge}

The electromagnetic charge of a particle defines how strongly it couples to an
external (electro-)magnetic field. As established before, the gravitational 
influence on a \CR particle can be safely ignored due to its' miniscule mass.
Consequentially, only \EM-fields and the corresponding coupling parameter, the
electric charge $q$, are of importance when considering the \CRs movement 
through the cosmos. In particular, the rigidity of a relativistic charged 
particle (given in units of Volts) is defined as the ratio between energy and 
charge of a particle:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rigidity}
R = \frac{E}{q}. 
\end{equation}

Physically, a high value of $R$ can represent different scenarios. 
Mathematically though, a smaller charge or higher energy can result in the same
value for rigidity. Differences in charge can be offset by differences in the 
(energy-dependent) inertia and vice versa. Investigating the appropriate 
equations of motion reveals that constant fractions of $E/q$ undergo equal
acceleration, implying that fundamentally, particles of equal rigidity behave 
equally in \EM-fields.

As the name implies, particles with high values of $R$ propagate through the 
same $\vec{B}$ field more rigidly than low-rigidity particles. Barring 
unphysical scenarios, neutral particles are the only candidates with 
$R=\SI{0}{\volt}$, and that are not deflected.

\subsection{Implications for Sources}

The next chapter discusses possible accelerator mechanisms of \CRs. Not much is
known with certainty about their inner workings. Nevertheless, a lot can be 
learned about the nature of \CR sources from examining the distribution of \CR
observables measured on Earth. A list of key insights is given in the next 
paragraphs.

\subsubsection{Constraints from Energy Spectrum}

The \CR energy spectrum (\cref{fig:cr-spectrum}) is well described by a broken
power law. The fact that several sharp transitions between different spectral
indices $\gamma$ exists is remarkable. A distribution of \CR sources with 
vastly different characteristics could easily wash out the observed features. 
The presence of these breaks implies that the sources must be similar, possibly
relying on the same acceleration mechanisms.

\subsubsection{Constraints from Arrival Directions}

The arrival direction of \CRs on Earth is not uniformly distributed. This is 
(at lower energies) owed to the geomagnetic field 
\cite{barberEastWestAsymmetryLatitude1949}. But even at higher energies large 
scale anisotropies exist. \cref{fig:dipole} shows the deviation of the observed
\CR flux above \SI{8}{\exa\eV} from the isotropic expectation. A top-hat filter 
with a width of $\SI{45}{\degree}$ is applied to the data as described in 
\cite{thepierreaugercollaborationObservationLargescaleAnisotropy2017}. A dipole
roughly perpendicular to the \acf{SGP} is clearly visible. If \CR sources are 
scattered throughout the Universe uniformly then this observation implies that 
the most energetic \CR accelerators lie outside of the milky way.

\subsubsection{Constraints from Mass Composition}

Observations of the shower depth from the Pierre Auger observatory hint towards
the most energetic cosmic rays being heavy, whereas light and heavy particles 
constitute the \CR population at intermediate energies 
\cite{mayotteMeasurementInterpretationUHECR2025}. These measurements support 
the conclusions made in the previous paragraphs and paint a consistent picture 
of the nature of \CRs, their origins and accelerator mechanisms;

The cosmic ray spectrum is made up of a galactic and an extragalactic 
component. Both components in turn can be divided into a population of light 
and heavy \CRs. The sources, following a Peters cycle 
\cite{petersPrimaryCosmicRadiation1961}, accelerate the particles to a cutoff
energy $E_\mathrm{max}\propto Z$. Extragalactic sources can have a higher
cutoff energy than those within the milky way. This explains the mixed 
composition of \CRs at intermediate energies. The \CR spectrum at energies up 
to $\sim\SI{1}{\exa\eV}$ consists of the heavy galactic component and the light
extragalactic component. In contrast, cosmic rays at higher energies originate
from outside the milky way and tend towards heavier particles.

\newpage
\section{Origins of Cosmic Rays}
\label{sec:cr-accelerators}

\halflift

\subsection{General Principles}
\label{ssec:hillas-criterion}

It has been established on the previous pages that \CR sources rely on similar,
if not the same principles to accelerate particles up to extreme energies. The
physical mechanism powering these processes must be the \EM force, as it is the
only interaction of sufficient strength at astrophysical length scales. All 
\CR source models thusrequire a strong electric or magnetic field which 
particles can interact with and gain energy from. For magnetic fields in 
particular an important criterion applies. Because 
$W_\mathrm{L}=\vec{F}_\mathrm{L}\cdot\vec{r}\propto\vec{v}\times\vec{r} = 0$, 
the magnetic force acts purely adiabatically in the magnetostatic case. It 
follows that the acceleration of a test particle with charge $q$ can only occur 
if the source magnetic field lines are evolving over time. This is most easily 
realized by rotating magnetic fields (of strength $\vec{B}$ and extent 
$L_{\vec{B}}$, rotating at a velocity $\vec{v}$), fueled e.g.\ by a dynamo 
effect \cite{straussDynamoEffectFusion1985}. A heuristic argument can then be 
made about the maximum energy $E_\mathrm{max}$ that a given accelerator can 
transfer to the test particle:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:hillas-criterion}
\frac{|\vec{B}|}{\unit{\upmu\gauss}}\,\frac{L_{\vec{B}}}{\unit{\parsec}} > 2\,\frac{E_\mathrm{max}}{\unit{\peta\eV}}\,\frac{e}{q}\,\frac{v}{c}   
\end{equation}

The reasoning for this, as laid out in 
\cite{hillasOriginUltraHighEnergyCosmic1984}, is simple; The test particle will
be accelerated while it remains in the magnetic field of the source. This leads
to a steady increase in the energy and rigidity of the particle. With an
increase in rigidity the Larmor radius $R_\mathrm{L} = E\,/\,(qc|\vec{B}|)$
grows. \cref{eq:hillas-criterion} roughly marks the energy at which the Larmor 
radius exceeds the size of the source magnetic field, and the test particle 
must necessarily escape. The magnetic field strength is not sufficient to trap 
the particle for further acceleration.

This so-called Hillas criterion provides an easy way of grouping accelerators 
based on their size and field strength. It also reveals that \CR sources have
such similar spectra precisely because they are comparable in size and field 
strength. \cref{fig:hillas-plot} shows different classes of \CR accelerators 
that can be grouped in this way. 

Moving on, this section focuses on the two source candidates that are thought 
to be the dominating contributors for galactic- and extragalactic \CRs, as well
as the indirect production of uncharged \CRs.

\subsection{Supernova Remnants}
\label{ssec:supernova-remnants}

\SNRs are, as the name implies, what remains of massive stars after they meet 
their violent end in a supernova. Whether a supernova is triggered by thermal 
runaway or a core collapse, the stellar explosion releases a tremendous amount 
of energy.

For thermonuclear supernovae a self-reinforcing fusion reaction is thought to 
increase the stars' temperature thousandfold in the span of a few seconds 
\cite{khokhlovLightCurvesType1993}. This rapid increase in internal energy far
surpasses the gravitational binding energy. The luminosity of the system 
rapidly increases and the star disintegrates.

On the contrary, core-collapse supernovae have the vast majority
($>\SI{90}{\percent}$) of the energy escape immediately in the form of 
neutrinos \cite{odrzywolekProbingThermonuclearSupernova2011}. The burst of 
weakly interacting particles is so immense that the escape blows off the outer 
layers of the star in the process, leaving behind only the core of the 
progenitor star \cite{walchEnergyMomentumInput2015}.

In both cases a shockwave of plasma is formed that moves at a small fraction
of the speed of light into the surrounding \ISM. Its' internal conductivity is 
infinite, as the plasma potential is the same everywhere within the shock 
front. It follows that internal magnetic field lines must be comoving within 
the plasma. Such moving magnetic fields provide an excellent condition for the 
genesis of cosmic rays.

\subsubsection{Diffusive Shock Acceleration}

\DSA describes the process by which a particle can gain energy and eventually 
escape as a cosmic ray from interactions with the magnetic fields of the shock 
front. For a mathematical description of the process consider the scenario 
shown in \cref{fig:fermi-type-i}; A \SNR with a magnetic field $\vec{B}$,
expanding with some velocity $\beta = |\vec{v}|/c$ into which a particle 
enters. Neither $\vec{B}$ nor $\beta$ are, in the general case, isotropic or 
constant.

$E_0 \approx p_0c$ and $E_1 \approx p_1c$ denote the energy of the particle 
before and after traversing the shock front.\footnote{$E_0$ is not the rest 
mass of the particle, but its (relativistic) energy upon entering the mag. 
field.} The flow direction of the shock front determines a reference system by 
which one can describe the trajectory of the particle. Accordingly, $\theta_0$ 
and $\theta_1$ mark the entrance and exit angles with the shock front 
respectively.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.36\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/hillas_plot.png}
  \label{fig:hillas-plot}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.36\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/diffusive_shock_acceleration.png}
  \label{fig:fermi-type-i}}
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:hillas-plot} Mean size and field strength for several 
  \CR accelerator candidates and their resulting maximum energy according to 
  \cref{eq:hillas-criterion} for $q=e$ and $v/c=0.5$. The top axis gives a 
  reference distance from Earth to some celestial objects. 
  \subref{fig:fermi-type-i} A particle with energy $E_0$ enters a \SNR (moving 
  on average at a velocity $\beta c$) at an angle $\theta_0$. It scatters 
  collisionless off of irregularities in the magnetic field and exits the \SNR 
  at an angle $\theta_1$ with energy $E_1$. The background image shows SN1604,
  from \cite{patnaudeOriginKeplersSupernova2012}.}
  \label{fig:fermi-acceleration}
\end{figure}

All above quantities are defined w.r.t.\ a global rest frame. In the barycenter
of the \SNR the particle energy is expressed as

\begin{equation}
E_0' = E_0\,\frac{\left( 1 + \beta\cos\theta_0 \right)}{\sqrt{1-\beta^2}}.
\end{equation}

Because of the static field lines in the \SNR barycenter, it is $E_0' = E_1'$.
Transforming back to the unprimed system 
($E_1=E_1'\,\left(1+\beta\cos\theta_1'\right)\,/\,\sqrt{1-\beta^2}$), 

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:fractional-energy-change}
\qquad\xi = \frac{E_1 - E_0}{E_0} = \frac{1 
	+ \beta\left(\cos\theta_0 + \cos\theta_1'\right) 
	+ \beta^2 \cos\theta_0\,\cos\theta_1'}{1-\beta^2} - 1. 
\end{equation}

The trivial cases $\beta=0$, $\theta_0 = \theta_1'=\pi$ yield a fractional 
change in energy $\xi=0$ as expected. In the general case, one can average over
the incident and reflection angle as done in e.g.\ 
\cite{gaisserCosmicRaysParticle2016} to retrieve a mean enery change 
$\langle\xi\rangle$. Doing so, an edge case of the magnetic scattering off the 
cloud becomes apparent;

For a sufficiently large \SNR the scattering occurs at a shock front that is
essentially plane. This changes the mean geometry, specifically

\begin{equation}
	\langle\cos\theta_0\rangle_\mathrm{cloud} = -\frac{\beta}{3}, 
	\,\,\mathrm{and}\qquad\qquad 
	\langle\cos\theta_1'\rangle_\mathrm{cloud} = 0,
\end{equation}

while

\begin{equation}
	\langle\cos\theta_0\rangle_\mathrm{plane} = -\frac{2}{3},
	\,\,\mathrm{and}\qquad\qquad 
	\langle\cos\theta_1'\rangle_\mathrm{plane} = \frac{2}{3}.
\end{equation}

The mean fractional change in energy for the two scenarios becomes thus

\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:fermi-energy-gain}
	\langle\xi\rangle_\mathrm{cloud} \sim \frac{4}{3}\,\beta^2, 
	\qquad 
	\langle\xi\rangle_\mathrm{plane} \sim \frac{4}{3}\,\beta.
\end{equation}

Since $0\leq\beta<1$, the mean fractional change in energy is strictly positive
in either case. This implies that on average the particle gains energy from the
interactions. Moreover, it gains more energy from the interaction with the 
plane front. Considering the geometry in \cref{fig:fermi-type-i}, the process 
must be a (isotropic) reflection, with $\cos\theta_0<0$ and $\cos\theta_1'>0$. 
As such, the linear term in $\beta$ remains, unlike for the cloud scattering, 
for which the leading term is only quadratic in $\beta$.

The two processes are known as type I (plane) and type II (cloud) Fermi
acceleration, after the discovery of the effect by Enrico Fermi in 1949
\cite{fermiOriginCosmicRadiation1949}.

Following \cref{eq:fermi-energy-gain}, a single interaction with the shock 
front can roughly double the energy of the incident particle, but not more. In 
order to increase its energy substantially the particle must cross the shock
front many times. After $n$ crossings the energy of the particle reads on 
average 

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:energy-per-crossing}
\langle E_n \rangle = E_0\left( 1 + \langle\xi\rangle \right)^n. 
\end{equation}

However, with each crossing there also exists a probability $P_\mathrm{esc.}$, 
corresponding to the particle escaping instead of entering the accelerator for 
an $n+1$th crossing. Heuristically, $P_\mathrm{esc.}$ should increase with 
larger $n$, as this corresponds to higher $E$, and larger Larmor radii. In a 
first order approximation of a constant escape probability the mean number of 
particles above a given energy thus behaves like 

\begin{equation}
	\langle N(\geq E_n)\rangle \sim \sum\limits_{m=n}^\infty 
	\left( 1 + P_\mathrm{esc.}\right)^m 
	= \frac{\left( 1-P_\mathrm{esc.} \right)^n}{P_\mathrm{esc.}}
	\propto \left(\frac{E}{E_0}\right)^{-\gamma}.
\end{equation}

The source spectra of particles accelerated by \SNRs thus follow a power law 
with spectral index $\gamma=\log\left(\frac{1}{1-P_\mathrm{esc.}}\right)\,/\,
\log\left(1+\langle\xi\rangle\right)$. For type II acceleration, the numeric 
value of $\gamma$ is determined by the cloud composition and extent, and tends 
to be very soft ($\gamma\gg1$) \cite{davisModifiedFermiMechanism1956}. For type
I acceleration, the spectral index can be rewritten in terms of the Mach number
$M=\beta c/v_\mathrm{sound}^\mathrm{ISM}$ of the shock front
\cite{gaisserCosmicRaysParticle2016}.

\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:snr-spectral-index}
	\gamma \approx 1 + \frac{4}{M^2}
\end{equation}

Assuming strong shocks ($M\gg1$), \cref{eq:snr-spectral-index} is well 
approximated by $\gamma\approx1.7$, which, after accounting for diffusion 
effects ($\gamma_\mathrm{Earth}\approx\gamma_\mathrm{Source}+1$), matches the 
energy spectrum observed on earth below the knee almost perfectly 
\cite{baloghPhysicsCollisionlessShocks2013}.

\subsubsection{Injection \& Escape}
\label{sssec:snr-injection-and-escape}

In order for acceleration to occur the \CR-to-be must enter the regime of 
moving magnetic fields, and cross into the shock front. As laid out in 
\cite{baloghPhysicsCollisionlessShocks2013} this requires a kinetic energy that
surpasses the thermal motion of particles in the \ISM ahead of the shock. There
is thus some need for a mechanism that boosts the energy of particles in the 
\ISM such that they can be injected into the accelerator.

While many injection models exist (e.g.\ Ref.\ 
\cite{galeevInjectionMechanismCosmic1989}), the precise mechanism by which 
injection is achieved remains unclear, giving rise to the injection problem 
\cite{baloghPhysicsCollisionlessShocks2013}. In any case, data shows that a 
reliable injection mechanism must exist, and particles can and do cross the 
shock front. 

Because $\frac{\mathrm{d}\beta}{\mathrm{dt}}<0$, the maximum energy that a 
given \CR can attain per crossing after injection is changing as the \SNR 
evolves. Moreover, the time per crossing, $T_\mathrm{Cycle}$, increases because
the \SNR is expanding ($\frac{\mathrm{d}L}{\mathrm{dt}}>0$) as it ages.

The maximum energy $E_\mathrm{max}$ attainable by a test particle can be
calculated by inspecting \cref{eq:energy-per-crossing}. It follows

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:energy-budget}
\frac{\mathrm{d}E}{\mathrm{dt}} = \frac{\langle\xi\rangle\cdot E}{T_\mathrm{Cycle}}.
\end{equation}

The cycle duration consists of an inscattering phase (in the upstream region of
the shock), and an outscattering phase (downstream, after the shock). In both 
cases, the particle propagates in a quasi-random manner based on the 
irregularities present in the \EM fields near the \SNR. Following 
\cite{lagageMaximumEnergyCosmic1983} this implies

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:snr-cycle}
T_\mathrm{Cycle} = \frac{4}{c^2} \left( \frac{D_\mathrm{upstream}}{\beta_\mathrm{upstream}} + \frac{D_\mathrm{downstream}}{\beta_\mathrm{downstream}} \right).
\end{equation}

During the free expansion phase of the \SNR the flow velocities remain nearly 
constant. Thus, efficient acceleration is achieved for small diffusion 
coefficients $D_i$. A lower limit for $D_i$ is given by the Larmor radius 
$R_L$, as the particle cannot be deflected by magnetic fields through which 
it moves balistically \cite{lagageMaximumEnergyCosmic1983}. With 
$D_\mathrm{min} = \frac{R_L\,c}{3}$ and again assuming a strong shock 
($\beta_\mathrm{upstream} = 4\beta_\mathrm{downstream}$), integrating 
\cref{eq:energy-budget} to the beginning of the Sedov-Taylor phase leads to

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:snr-max-energy}
E_\mathrm{max} \leq \frac{3}{20} \int\limits_0^{T_\mathrm{ST}}\langle\xi\rangle q c^2 \left|\vec{B}\right| \beta_\mathrm{upstream}\, \mathrm{dE}.
\end{equation}

Interestingly, this leads to a maximum energy that only depends on the charge 
of the injected particle, not its initial energy. Inserting realistic 
parameters from observations, the maximum energy for \SNRs according to 
\cref{eq:snr-max-energy} is \SI{1}{\peta\eV}, in line with  predictions by the 
Hillas-criterion (Ref.\ \cref{fig:hillas-plot}).

\subsection{Active Galactic Nuclei}
\label{ssec:active-galactic-nuclei}

Most (if not all) large galaxies host a \SMBH at their center
\cite{kormendyInwardBoundSearch1995, kormendyCoevolutionNotSupermassive2013}. 
A subsection of galaxies further host a central region whose luminosity far 
outshines the rest of the galactic body. Following the emission activity of thegalactic core, these structures are called \AGNs.

The processes behind the massive energy discharge cannot be of stellar nature,
as the observed luminosity exceeds the Eddington limit 
\cite{rybickiRadiativeProcessesAstrophysics2024}. Instead, gravitational 
collapse fueled by the immense gravitational pull of the \SMBH is theorized to 
power the emission. For example, particles can scatter inelastically in the 
accretion disk surrounding the \SMBH. The gravitational potential of one 
particle is passed onto a second particle which escapes the central region at a
higher energy. Such processes can be enhanced by the presence of strong 
magnetic fields \cite{piotrovichDeterminationMagneticField2020}.
 
\subsubsection{Electromagnetic Centrifugal Acceleration}
\label{sssec:centrifugal-acceleration}

In particular, rotating magnetic field lines (caused by a Reissner-Nordström 
singularity) force charged particles orbiting at a distance $r$ to
undergo centrifugal acceleration. This leads to a resonant interaction in which
rotational energy from the \SMBH, rotating with angular velocoty $\Omega$, is 
transferred to the particle like 

\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:centrifugal-acceleration}
	E(r) = \frac{E_0}{1-\left(\frac{\Omega r}{c}\right)^2},
\end{equation}

as calculated in \cite{riegerParticleAccelerationRotating1999}. The fact that 
$\lim\limits_{\Omega r\to c^-} E(r) = \infty$ implies that the outspiraling 
must be physically stopped at some point. This is most likely achieved by a 
strong photon background resulting from gravitational friction above and beyond
the accretion disk. In such environments accelerating particles lose energy via
\ICS, limiting the maximum attainable energy of (direct) centrigfugal 
acceleration in \AGNs to $\mathcal{O}(\unit{\exa\eV})$ 
\cite{osmanovEfficiencyParticleAcceleration2007}.

\subsubsection{Langmuir Damping \& Wave Collapse}

The plasma surrounding the \AGN can function as a catalyst for a very 
efficienct acceleration of cosmic rays 
\cite{osmanovExtremelyEfficientZevatron2014}. Expanding from 
\cref{eq:centrifugal-acceleration}, it is evident that electrons, due to their
different rest mass, undergo a different centrifugal acceleration from ions. 
This results in a large-scale charge separation within the plasma, and the 
formation of Langmuir waves surrounding the \SMBH. 

Initially, the plasma waves have a large wavelength, and, because 
$v_\mathrm{Phase} \propto \omega/\lambda$, a very high phase velocity that
exceeds the speed of light. Since the charge carriers cannot match this 
velocity, Landau damping (Ref.\ \cite{landau61VibrationsElectronic1965}) 
cannot occur. The process continues uninterrupted and rotational energy of the 
magnetic field is transferred into plasma wave energy, the amplitudes of the 
Langmuir waves grow.

This is only stopped once the amplitude is big enough to result in the collapse
of low-pressure cavities doe to wave-driven pressure differences. The plasma 
wavelength rapidly decreases because of the cave-in and the phase velocity 
becomes sub-luminal. Suddenly, an overlap between particle speeds and the 
plasma phase velocity enables Landau damping, and wave energy is passed onto 
the charge carriers. For protons in particular the energy after collapse 
becomes

\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:langmuir-acceleration}
	E \approx 6.4\times10^{112}\,\, \frac{f^3}{\gamma_2^5} \,\,
	\sqrt{\left(
		\frac{M_\odot}{M_\mathrm{AGN}}
		\frac{L_\mathrm{AGN}}{\unit{erg\per\second}} 
	      \right)^5}\,\mathrm{eV}
\end{equation}

according to \cite{osmanovExtremelyEfficientZevatron2014}. In 
\cref{eq:langmuir-acceleration} $f=\delta n/n_0$ denotes the magnitude of 
irregularities $\delta n$ deviating from the nominal plasma density $n_0$. 
$\gamma_2$ is the Lorentz factor of the ion plasma stream, $M_\mathrm{AGN}$ and
$L_\mathrm{AGN}$ are the mass and luminosity of the \AGN respectively. 
Observations of the plasma conditions in \AGNs place a realistic upper limit
of this acceleration mechanism at 
$\mathcal{O}(\unit{Z\eV} = \SI{1e21}{\eV})$.

\subsection{Indirect Production}
\label{ssec:indirect-production}

Whereas particles with a charge of zero cannot be accelerated via the discussed
mechanisms, neutral \CRs are observed in nature 
\cite{lhaasocollaborationUltrahighenergyRayBubble2024,
aielloObservationUltrahighenergyCosmic2025}. They must be created in a 
secondary, indirect fashion, where a high-energy charged \CR can transfer 
(part of) its' energy to a secondary, uncharged particle via interactions 
either close to the source (Ref.\ \cref{sssec:synchrotron-emission}) or during 
propagation (Ref.\ \cref{sssec:deep-inelastic-scattering}).

\subsubsection{Synchrotron Emission}
\label{sssec:synchrotron-emission}

\CR electrons, because of their low mass, are especially prone to acceleration 
via \EM fields, both near the source, and during propagation via the \GMF. Near
the source in particular, the charged particles often move relativistically in 
a circular pattern (c.f.\ \cref{sssec:centrifugal-acceleration}). Under such
circumstances the (by the electron) isotropically emitted Bremsstrahlung is 
boosted into the direction of the electron motion. The brilliance and
thus intensity of the light beam increases with the Lorentz factor of the 
electron \cite{rybickiRadiativeProcessesAstrophysics2024}. This is called 
synchrotron radiation, and is an important production mechanism for photons 
with energies up to few \unit{\kilo\eV} \cite{gaisserCosmicRaysParticle2016} 
around e.g.\ pulsars, or accreting black holes.

\subsubsection{Deep inelastic scattering}
\label{sssec:deep-inelastic-scattering}

Deep inelastic scattering as studied at the \LHC also occurs readily in nature
when \CRs interact with the \ISM. The invariant mass of the colliding system
often exceeds that of the \LHC ($\SI{14}{\tera\eV}$) by many orders of 
magnitude. 

In the simplest case a high-energy proton interacts with ambient (stationary) 
matter. This predominantly produces light pions that decay into photons.

\vspace{0.5cm}
\begin{centering}
	
	\begin{tikzpicture}
		\node(initial_state){$p_\mathrm{CR} + \mathrm{gas}\,$};
		\node(final_state1) [above right =-0.3em and 2em of initial_state]{$\,X + \pi^0 \, \longrightarrow X + \gamma\gamma$};
		\node(final_state2) [right =2em of initial_state]{$\,X + \pi^+ \longrightarrow X + \mu^+ + \nu_\mu$};
		\node(final_state3) [below right =-0.3em and 2em of initial_state]{$\,X + \pi^- \longrightarrow X + \mu^- + \bar{\nu}_\mu$};
		\draw [->] (initial_state.east) to [out=0, in=180] (final_state1.west);
	    	\draw [->] (initial_state.east) to [out=0, in=180] (final_state2.west);
		\draw [->] (initial_state.east) to [out=0, in=180] (final_state3.west);
	\end{tikzpicture}

\end{centering}
\vspace{0.5cm}

The blue band in \cref{fig:photon-limits} shows the photon flux expected from 
this model.

In a similar fashion, the production of charged pions result in neutrinos in 
the final state. Their energy behaves like 
$\langle E_\nu\rangle=E_{\pi^\pm}-E_{\mu\pm}\approx\frac{E_{\pi^\pm}}{1000}$. 
As before, for the right $|X\rangle$ this expression can approach the primary 
particles energy $E_\mathrm{CR}$. Since the neutrinos are created as a 
secondary during the propagation of primary \CRs they are called cosmogenic 
neutrinos. \cref{fig:neutrino-limits} shows the expected flux of different 
astrophysical models that predict cosmogenic neutrinos.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.36\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/photon_limits.png}
  \label{fig:photon-limits}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.36\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/neutrino_limits.png}
  \label{fig:neutrino-limits}
  }
  \caption[]{Expected flux of high-energy $\gamma$'s \subref{fig:photon-limits}
  and $\nu$'s \subref{fig:neutrino-limits}. Data from
  \cite{niechciolLatestResultsSearches2024}.}
  \label{fig:neutral-limits}
\end{figure}
	
\subsubsection{Greisen-Zatsepin-Kuzmin Limit}
\label{sssec:gzk-cutoff}

The mean free path of \CRs is not significantly reduced by the interactions 
described in \cref{sssec:deep-inelastic-scattering}. This changes drastically
once the \GZK threshold is crossed. At an energy $>\SI{50}{\exa\eV}$ the 
invariant mass of a proton \CR and a \CMB photon exceeds \SI{1232}{\mega\eV\per c^2}, this is enough to produce a spin-excitation of the proton.

\vspace{0.5cm}
\begin{centering}
	
	\begin{tikzpicture}
		\node(initial_state){$p_\mathrm{CR} + \gamma_\mathrm{CMB}\longrightarrow \Delta^+\,$};
		\node(final_state1) [above right =-0.5em and 2em of initial_state]{$\,p + \pi^0$};
		\node(final_state2) [below right =-0.5em and 2em of initial_state]{$\,n + \pi^+$};
		\draw [->] (initial_state.east) to [out=0, in=180] (final_state1.west);
	    	\draw [->] (initial_state.east) to [out=0, in=180] (final_state2.west);
	\end{tikzpicture}

\end{centering}
\vspace{0.5cm}

With the \CMB number density measuring roughly \SI{400}{\per\centi\meter\cubed}
\cite{adamPlanck2015Results2016} it is thought that photon \CRs exceeding the 
\GZK threshold cannot freely propagate in the universe without losing a 
considerable amount of energy through this interaction. The mean free path 
shrinks to several \unit{\mega\parsec} \cite{greisenEndCosmicRaySpectrum1966}, 
implying that photons above the \GZK threshold energy must have been produced 
locally.

A different loss model, called the \GDR 
\cite{steckerPhotodisintegrationUltrahighEnergyCosmic1969}, suggests that this
happens likewise for heavier particles at comparable energies.

\section{Cosmic Ray Propagation}
\label{sec:cr-propagation}

The propagation of cosmic rays refers to their galactic and extragalactic 
transport from a source region to Earth. This cosmic play occurs over 
timescales measuring hundreds of thousands of years. The stage through which 
the \CR actors propagate is set up by various magnetic fields, as presented in 
\cref{ssec:cosmic-magnetic-fields}. Different effects that can occur during the
transport are highlighted in \cref{ssec:galactic-transport}.

\subsection{Cosmic Magnetic Fields}
\label{ssec:cosmic-magnetic-fields}

\halflift

\subsubsection{Coherent Galactic Component}

The milky way, like many spiral galaxies \cite{beckMagneticFieldsSpiral2016}, 
supports an orderly magnetic field that permeates the entire galaxy. This 
coherent component of the \GMF is thought to originate from primordial seed 
fields that are amplified by processes such as small scale dynamos 
\cite{kimInterstellarTurbulenceDriving2006}. The galactic evolution then 
follows these seed fields. Newly formed structures tend to align with the field
lines, and a self-reinforcing process kicks off that leads to large-scale \EM 
fields with strength of several \unit{\upmu\gauss} 
\cite{wangMagnetohydrodynamicSimulationsDisk2009, 
kotarbaMagneticFieldStructure2009, gresselHybridDynamoModel2013}.
\cref{fig:coherent-magnetic-field} shows the magnetic flux density of the 
coherent galactic magnetic field.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.37\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/coherent_galactic_field.png}
  \label{fig:coherent-magnetic-field}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.37\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/flux_demagnification.png}
  \label{fig:flux-demagnification}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:coherent-magnetic-field} The coherent magnetic field 
	     strength along the galactic plane. The parametrization along with 
	     used parameters displayed here is the \texttt{neCL} model 
	     presented in \cite{ungerCoherentMagneticField2024}. The position 
	     of the solar neighborhood is marked with a star. 
     	     \subref{fig:flux-demagnification} The comparison between true
     	     (solid points) and perceived (hollow points) arrival direction of 
	     \CRs on earth after traversing the \GMF for a single simulation.
	     Particle trajectories are calculated using \CRs with a rigidity of
	     \SI{10}{\exa\volt} in \texttt{CRPropa} 
	     \cite{batistaCRPropa32Advanced2022}, using the \texttt{JF12}-\GMF
	     field model \cite{janssonGalacticMagneticField2012}. The shaded 
	     region gives the distribution of true arrival directions that 
	     arises from using different seeds for the turbulent component of 
     	     the \GMF.}
  \label{fig:magnetic-field}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Turbulent Galactic Component}

The turbulent component of the \GMF is the difference between the ordered 
component discussed above and the total \GMF. Its' coherence length measures 
typically $L_\mathrm{C} \leq \SI{5}{\parsec}$ 
\cite{giacintiConstraintsPropertiesTurbulent2019}, and can be separated into 
many individual Weissian domains that are oriented in a quasi-random manner.
With field strenghts up to \SI{6}{\upmu\gauss} 
\cite{hanSpatialEnergySpectrum2004} the turbulent \GMF represents a 
considerable fraction of the total galactic magnetic field, stronger than the 
coherent component in many regions. The chaotic orientation complicates \CR 
propagation to a (transient) random walk in three dimensions. 
\cref{fig:flux-demagnification} shows the flux demagnification of \CR arrival 
directions due to the \GMF and its' turbulent component in particular.

\subsubsection{Extragalactic Component}

The \EGMF influences the transport of \CRs between galaxies. Given the 
relatively weak (estimated) strength of the \EGMF (ranging from 
$\sim\SI{1}{\nano\gauss}$ \cite{brayUpperLimitStrength2018} to 
$\sim\SI{1}{\upmu\gauss}$ \cite{valleeDetectingLargestMagnets1990}), as well as
the relatively high rigidity of extragalactic \CRs (Ref.\ \cref{sssec:energy}),
it is usually assumed that \CRs travel balistically in-between galaxies;
Interactions with the \EGMF are neglected. 

This has implications for the identification and search of extragalactic 
sources of \UHECRs. One example of this is the Amaterasu particle
\cite{telescopearraycollaborationExtremelyEnergeticCosmic2023}, an \EAS with an
associated reconstructed primary energy of $E\approx\SI{240}{\exa\eV}$. 
Neglecting the \EGMF, backtracking algorithms like 
\cite{batistaCRPropa32Advanced2022} place the most probable origin of the 
primary particle in the local void \cite{ungerWhereDidAmaterasu2024}, a region 
sparse in \UHECR sources \cite{tullyNearbyGalaxiesCatalog1988}, and require 
transient production mechanisms which are not yet thoroughly understood at the 
highest energies \cite{farrarBinaryNeutronStar2025}. Meanwhile, assuming a 
non-zero \EGMF, \cite{bourricheLocalVoidComprehensive2024}\todo{change to updated version} constrains the source location to 
a region with several candidates that can produce particles at the required
energies. The question whether the \EGMF plays a considerable role in \CR 
propagation is unanswered, and requires a better understanding of their 
dynamics.

\subsection{Galactic Transport}
\label{ssec:galactic-transport}

The transport of \CRs in the milky way is goverened by many different 
processes. In general, these processes are of stochastic nature. The 
examination of the trajectory of a single \CR thus only gives an incomplete 
picture of the many phenomena that can occur during transport. It is more 
instructive to consider a population of \CRs (of type $i$) and study how the 
entire population evolves in space and time. Without loss of generality, it is 
evident that the (diffusive) magnetic transport along the turbulent component 
of the \GMF conserves the number of particles $\mathcal{N}_i$,

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:particle-conservation}
\frac{\partial\mathcal{N}_i}{\partial t} - \nabla \cdot \bigg[\,\widetilde{D}_i(E,\vec{r}, t)\,\nabla\mathcal{N}_i\,\bigg] = 0.
\end{equation}

$\widetilde{D}_i(E,\vec{r}, t)$ in \cref{eq:particle-conservation} gives the 
directionally depependent diffusion coefficients at point $\vec{r}$ and time 
$t$ for particles of type $i$. Because the driving force powering the diffusion
is the Lorentz force, $\widetilde{D}$ is rigidity- and thus energy-dependent, 
with higher energy \CRs diffusing quicker than lower energy \CRs.

The diffusion only models the adiabatic transport of preexisting cosmic rays.
Their creation can be described by adding a source term $Q_i(E, \vec{r}, t)$. 
For example, the prompt emission of \UHE photons during a \GRB can be encoded 
as

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:grb-source-term}
Q_\gamma(E, \vec{r}, t) = N_\gamma(E) \, \delta(\vec{r}-\vec{r}_\mathrm{GRB}) \, \delta(t-t_\mathrm{GRB}).
\end{equation}

During transport of particles of type $i$ the production of secondary \CRs of 
type $j\neq i$ can occur (Ref.\ \cref{ssec:indirect-production}), the 
production rate is tied to the scattering cross section $\sigma_{ij}$ 
describing the interaction $p_\mathrm{ISM} + \CR_i \rightarrow X + \CR_j$
\footnote{For weakly unstable primaries decay can occur in vacuum as well.}, 
the initial \CR is destroyed in the process and $\mathcal{N}_i$ decreases as a 
consequence.

This also happens in reverse, and \CRs of type $i$ are produced in secondary 
interactions. In equilibrium, the population size during transport remains 
constant, implying that globally

\begin{align}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{N}_i}{\partial t} &= \int Q_i \, \mathrm{d}\vec{r} \nonumber \\
					    &\qquad + \rho_\mathrm{ISM} \, \sum\limits_{j\neq i} \Bigg(\underbrace{\int \frac{\mathrm{d}\sigma_{ji}(E, E')}{\mathrm{d}E} |\vec{v}_j| \, \mathcal{N}_j \, \mathrm{d}E' \vphantom{\Bigg[}}_\mathrm{Secondary\,production} - \underbrace{\sigma_{ij}(E) \, |\vec{v}_i|\, \mathcal{N}_i \vphantom{\Bigg[}}_\mathrm{Primary\,destruction} \Bigg) \nonumber \\
					    &\qquad + \sum\limits_{j\neq i} \Bigg(\underbrace{\lambda_{ji}\,\mathcal{N}_j - \lambda_{ij}\,\mathcal{N}_i \vphantom{\Bigg[}}_\mathrm{Weak\,decay}\Bigg) \nonumber \\
					    &= 0. \label{eq:secondary-equilibrium}
\end{align}

Finally, \CRs can thermalize during transport via e.g.\ inelastic scattering
processes. An example of this is the interactions of \UHECRs with the \CMB 
covered in \cref{sssec:gzk-cutoff}. The energy loss models
$\frac{\partial k_i}{\partial E} $ must be tailored for each \CR type 
individually, as different primaries can posses different interaction channels.

Combining \cref{eq:particle-conservation} through 
(\ref{eq:secondary-equilibrium}), one arrives at a system of parabolic partial 
differential equations that are in practice solved numerically by tools such as
\texttt{CRPropa} \cite{batistaCRPropa32Advanced2022} or \texttt{GALPROP}
\cite{strongGALPROPCosmicRayPropagation2009}. \cref{fig:galactic-propagation} 
shows such calculated trajectories of particles of different rigidities in the 
milky way, as well as their perceived origin.

\halflift
\subsection{(Nested) Leaky Box Model}
\label{ssec:leaky-box-model}

One can in principle solve the equation of state analytically for extremely
simplified boundary conditions. This is done here for an illustrative example 
where all secondary interactions of \CRs during transport will be neglected.
\footnote{Meaning spallation, radioactive decay, and energy losses.} Due to the
oblate shape of the milky way escape from the galaxy occurs predominanly along
the $z$-axis. The parameters of interest are, following 
\cite{skillingDiffusionCosmicRays1970},

\begin{equation}
	Q(z, t) = \mathcal{N}_0 \, \delta(z)\delta(t), \qquad D_z = \frac{1}{3} \,\frac{\gamma|\vec{B}|L_{\vec{B}}}{\sqrt{\mu_0\rho_\mathrm{ISM}}},
\end{equation}

and a particular solution to this initial value problem is given by

\begin{equation}
	\mathcal{N}(z, t) = \frac{\mathcal{N}_0}{\sqrt{4\pi\,D_z t}} \, e^{-z^2/(2D_z t)}.
\end{equation}

The fraction of particles escaping the galaxy after time $t$ can be found by
integrating the \CR population along the height of the milky way. From this,
the expected time to escape for an individual \CR is calculated as

\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:escape-time}
	\langle\frac{1}{\tau_\mathrm{esc.}}\rangle = \int t \, r_\mathrm{esc}(t)\,\mathrm{d}t = \int t \, \left( 1 - \int\limits_{-H}^{H} \frac{\mathcal{N}(z, t)}{\mathcal{N}_0}\,\mathrm{d}z \right) \, \mathrm{d}t
\end{equation}

The full set of partial differential equations can be drastically simplified 
with a related Ansatz. The diffusion term, secondary production and destruction
are modeled stochastically via mean free path lengths and average lifetimes. 
The equation of state in equilibrium becomes

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:leaky-box}
\frac{\mathcal{N}_i}{\tau_\mathrm{esc.}} = Q_i - \left( \frac{|\vec{v}_i|\,\rho_\mathrm{ISM}}{\lambda_i} + \frac{1}{\gamma_i\tau_i}\right)\,\mathcal{N}_i + \sum\limits_{j\neq i} \left( \frac{|\vec{v}_j|\rho_\mathrm{ISM}}{\lambda_{ji}}  + \frac{1}{\gamma_j\tau_{ji}}\right)\,\mathcal{N}_j.
\end{equation}

This is the leaky box model \cite{cowsik3degKBlackbodyRadiation1966,
cowsikSteadyStateCosmicRay1967}, which can be extended by adding nested boxes 
of high density in the galaxy \cite{cowsikResidenceTimeCosmic1973, 
meneguzziEnergyDependencePrimary1973} that model \CR genitors.

In any case, the leaky box model serves as a reasonable approximation for a 
steady-state solution of \CR transport. \cref{fig:escape-comparison} shows the 
comparison between \cref{eq:escape-time} and numerical simulations.

A stark discrepancy between both models can be noted with increasing rigidity. 
This implies that the assumption of a completely diffusive propagation in 
\cref{eq:escape-time} does not hold at very high energies, where \CRs propagate
balistically instead.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/galactic_propagation_map.png}
  \label{fig:galactic-propagation}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/propagation_escape_comparison.png}
  \label{fig:escape-comparison}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:galactic-propagation} Example trajectories of 
	     particles with different rigidities obtained from numerical 
	     integration using \texttt{CRPropa}. The dashed lines give the 
	     perceived arrival direction on  Earth. Image from 
	     \cite{nasa/jpl-caltechOurMilkyWay2008} 
	     \subref{fig:escape-comparison} Comparison of escape trajectories 
	     in the leaky box model versus \texttt{CRPropa} simulations. 
	     $d_\mathrm{bal.}$ is the ballistic fraction of the particle 
	     trajectory, defined as the distance from initial position divided
     	     by the traversed path length.}
  \label{fig:propagation}
\end{figure}

\section{Extensive Air Showers}
\label{sec:extensive-air-showers}

The creation and transport of \CRs has been extensively discussed in
\cref{sec:cr-accelerators} and \cref{sec:cr-propagation} respectively. In this
section the final stage of the life of a cosmic ray in the upper atmosphere of 
the Earth is summarized. For this purpose, \cref{ssec:cascade-model} details
the formation and evolution of \EASs. The possible detection channels through
which \EASs can be observed are presented in \cref{ssec:detection-channels} and
onwards.

\subsection{Cascade Physics}
\label{ssec:cascade-model}

Whereas \CRs can propagate almost unhindered in space, crossing a column 
density of only $\lesssim\SI{8}{\gram\per\centi\meter\squared}$ on average
before entering the solar system \cite{gaisserCosmicRaysParticle2016}, this 
quickly changes upon entering the atmosphere of Earth. The mean free path of 
the \CR decreases drastically due to the high(er) density environment, and a 
deeply inelastic interaction takes place in which the primary particle is 
destroyed. 

The \UHE secondary particles, carrying the momentum of the impinging \CR, 
suffer a similar fate and also interact non-elastically with surrounding air 
molecules. A particle cascade kicks off that is only stopped once the primary 
energy is distributed to so many particles that they can thermalize in the
atmosphere.

\subsubsection{Heitler-Matthews-Model}
\label{sssec:heitler-matthews-model}

A simple but effective model to describe the particle avalanche is given by
\cite{matthewsHeitlerModelExtensive2005}. The evolution of the \EAS can thus be
separated into discrete steps. In a first step, the initial particle produces
$N_\pi$ pions, with the primary energy $E_0$ being shared roughly equally among
them. Since the branching ratios for neutral and charged pions are roughly 
equal, this amounts to $\frac{2N_\pi}{3}$ charged $\pi^\pm$ and 
$\frac{N_\pi}{3}$ uncharged $\pi^0$. 

The lifetime of the uncharged pions ($\mathcal{O}(\unit{\atto\second})$ 
\cite{nakamuraParticlePhysicsBooklet2010}) is much shorter than that of the 
charged pions ($\mathcal{O}(\unit{\nano\second})$ 
\cite{nakamuraParticlePhysicsBooklet2010}). They decay (quasi) instantly into 
two equi-energetic photons that carry their parents momentum forward.

Moving on, it is sensible to separate the \EAS into a hadronic and an \EM
component. The charged pions, after traversing an atmospheric depth equal to 
one nuclear interaction length $\lambda_\mathrm{int.}$ produce another $N_\pi$
pions each, where the uncharged portion immediately decays and the charged 
portion fuels the hadronic cascade.

The photons produced in the $\pi^0$ decays are responsible for driving the \EM 
component of the shower. After traversing close to one radiation length 
$\frac{7}{9} X_0$ ($\neq \lambda_\mathrm{int.}$) the high-energy photons 
undergo pair production, resulting in an $e^+ e^-$ pair, which, after 
traversing another $X_0$ produce a photon each via bremsstrahlung. Throughout 
all these processes it is assumed that the energy of the parent particle is 
equally distributed to the child particles. The schematic evolution is 
visualized in \cref{fig:heitler-matthews-cascade}.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  %\input{figures/cosmic-rays/heitler-matthews-model.tex}
  \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/heitler_matthews_model.png}
  \caption{Schematic evolution of an \EAS in the Heitler-Matthews model after a
  	   proton interacts inelastically in the upper atmosphere. Hadronic
   	   interaction lengths and radiation lengths are drawn in proportion.
   	   The saturation of tracks proxies their energy.}
  \label{fig:heitler-matthews-cascade}
\end{figure}

The number of particles in the cascade grows exponentially. After only five 
hadronic interaction lengths the number of particles in the shower surpasses 
$10^6$. 

Energy conservation requires that the energy per particle drops consequently. 
Eventually, the one-particle-energy drops below a critical energy 
($E^\mathrm{crit.}_\mathrm{EM}$ for $e^\pm$, and 
$\upgamma$; $E^\mathrm{crit.}_\mathrm{H}$ for hadronic particles), where 
ionization processes become the dominant interaction channel for the shower 
constituents. The cascade freezes out, the hadronic component decays to 
relativistic muons, and the \EM component is absorbed or thermalized. The 
shower evolution parameters that govern this are listed in \cref{tab:shower-parameters}.

The Heitler-Matthews model provides a conceptually simple treatment of the 
chaotic particle cascade. The generalizations function well in the high-entropy
state, with many particles present in the cascade. However, like any chaotic 
system, the evolution of the \EAS is extremely sensitive to initial conditions.
Deviations from the mean behaviour (i.e.\ $N_\pi = 20$ instead of $15$) get 
amplified in the early stages of the avalanche buildup, and result in large 
shower-to-shower fluctuations.

\subsubsection{Shower Observables}

The evolution of the shower is heavily stochastic. The shower-to-shower 
fluctuations discussed above make every \EAS distinct. Still, there exist 
commonalities. Every air shower is initiated by a single particle only, and 
quickly grows in size until the critical energy is reached. At roughly this 
moment the multiplicity of the \EAS records its highest value. The atmospheric 
depth at which this occurs, referred to as $\Xmax$, is similar for showers with
the same primary particles and similar energy. Given that 
$N(X) = 2^{\lfloor X/X_0 \rfloor}$ for the \EM component of the shower, and 
$E(X) = E_0 / N(X)$, it follows at the shower maximum 



\begin{table}[h!]
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.70\linewidth}

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:shower-maximum}
N_\mathrm{max} \propto E_0, \qquad\Xmax \propto \ln{E_0}.
\end{equation}

This relation is exact for electrically neutral particles 
\cite{HeitlerQuantumTheory}. While shower-to-shower fluctuations play a larger 
role for charged primaries, \cref{eq:shower-maximum} generalizes especially for
hadronic primaries as well \cite{gaisserCosmicRaysParticle2016,
matthewsHeitlerModelExtensive2005}.

Moving past characteristics of the primary particle (energy, inclination, and 
type), one parameter describing characteristics of the developing cascade is 
the shower age \cite{rossiCosmicRayTheory1941}, 

\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.25\linewidth}
\vspace{-0.1cm}
\caption{Evolution parameters proposed in 
\cite{matthewsHeitlerModelExtensive2005}.}
\label{tab:shower-parameters}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{cc}
\toprule
$N_\pi$ 		       & 15 \\
$\lambda_\mathrm{int.}$        & \SI{120}{\gram\per\centi\meter\squared} \\
$X_0$ 			       & \SI{37}{\gram\per\centi\meter\squared} \\
$E^\mathrm{crit.}_\mathrm{H}$  & \SI{20}{\giga\eV} \\
$E^\mathrm{crit.}_\mathrm{EM}$ & \SI{85}{\mega\eV} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}

\end{minipage}%
\end{table}

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:shower-age}
\bar{s}(X, \Xmax) = \frac{3X}{X + 2\Xmax}\ .
\end{equation}

Like the $x$-axis in \cref{fig:heitler-matthews-cascade}, $\bar{s}$ 
monotonically increases with the distance from the first interaction point.
Using this analogy, it is easy to see that energy distribution of particles is
almost entirely characterized by $\bar{s}$.\footnote{Dependencies on $E_0$ are
absorbed by defining the shower age in terms of $\Xmax$.} Similar connections 
are found for the angular and lateral distribution of particles in the \EAS
\cite{rossiCosmicRayTheory1941}, with both functions tending to broaden as the 
shower age increases.

\subsubsection{Hadronic Interaction Models}
\label{sssec:hadronic-interaction-models}

A feeling for the size of shower-to-shower fluctuations can be gathered from 
bootstrapping simulations of the same \CR primary particle interacting with the
atmosphere. Multiple codes and frameworks exist that aim to balance the 
physical accuracy of the reenactment versus the required computational power. A
(non-exhaustive) list of different models is given here in no particular order:

\begin{itemize}
\vspace{0.1cm}
\item \texttt{QGSJET-III} \cite{ostapchenkoMonteCarloTreatment2011} relies on 
extrapolating predictions of \QFTs to the highest energies for phenomena such 
as screening and multiple scattering. The models theoretical approach also has 
drawbacks. The complex calculations result in a time intensive simulation 
process. Moreover, the extrapolation of theoretical predictions is not perfect,
\texttt{QGSJET-II} predicts a lower number of muons than what is observed in 
air shower experiments.

\vspace{0.1cm}
\item \texttt{EPOS LHC} \cite{pierogEPOSLHCTest2015} (and the revised 
\texttt{EPOS LHC R} \cite{pierogEPOSLHCRGlobalApproach2025}) aims to accurately
model the central and forward particle production, that, while still 
underpredicting data by $\sim\SI{30}{\percent}$, result in more realistic muon 
yields. 

\vspace{0.1cm}
\item \texttt{Sibyll} \cite{riehnHadronicInteractionModel2017} is an efficient
\MC based simulation framework designed with tuneability in mind. The models
versatility makes it extremely useful in phenomenological studies of the muonic
component of \EASs.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{0.1cm}
\noindent\cref{fig:eas-simulations} compares \EAS particle counts between 
different hadronic interaction models. 

\subsection{Different Primaries}
\label{ssec:different-primaries}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.35\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/iron.png}
  \label{fig:eas-iron}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.35\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/proton.png}
  \label{fig:eas-proton}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.35\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/photon.png}
  \label{fig:eas-photon}
  }
  \caption[]{\texttt{CORSIKA} 8 \cite{engelNextGenerationCORSIKA2018, 
  alameddineSimulatingRadioEmission2025} simulations of an 
  \subref{fig:eas-iron} iron, \subref{fig:eas-proton} proton, and 
  \subref{fig:eas-photon} photon primary. The particle count at distance $r$ 
  from the travel axis of the primary particle is compared for different 
  primaries and different hadronic interaction models.}
  \label{fig:eas-simulations}
\end{figure}

The Heitler-Matthews model of \EASs in \cref{ssec:cascade-model} considers a 
proton as the primary particle. While the long range interactions during 
acceleration and transport are sensitive to the rigidity of the \CR only,
\footnote{Which is determined by the \textit{total} mass and \textit{total} 
charge} the destruction in the Earths atmosphere occurs at close range. As 
such, the exact nuclear composition of the primary is important. The resonant 
interaction of a e.g.\ iron core with air will look different than the 
deep-inelastic scattering of a proton. Of particular significance for this work
is the distinction of photon primaries from other \CRs (Ref.\ 
\cref{sssec:photon-showers}).

\subsubsection{Hadronic Particles}
\label{sssec:hadronic-primaries}

Hadronic primaries refer to \CR particles that are bound by the (residual) 
strong force. The lightest hadronic primaries observed in nature are thus 
hydrogen ions (i.e.\ protons) with an atomic number $Z=1$ for which the 
developing cascade has been described in \cref{ssec:cascade-model}. Showers 
initiated by heavier elements behave differently on average compared to the 
1-nucleon model. 

The average binding energy of nucleons is $\mathcal{O}(\unit{\mega\eV})$, and 
much lower than the interaction energies. Consequently, the \CR with a mass 
number $A$ can be treated as $A$ free hadrons, each with an energy $E_0/A$. The
\EAS resulting from the inelastic interaction of the primary is therefore a 
superposition of all 1-nucleon showers. 

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:superposition-model}
\Xmax^A \propto \ln E_0/A.
\end{equation}

Thus, the heavier the primary particle, the higher up in the atmosphere the 
\EAS develops, and the fewer splitting steps are needed until the shower 
maximum is reached. Following the Heitler-Matthews model the energy fraction 
contained in the hadronic and \EM component after $n_\mathrm{H}$ splittings is

\begin{equation}
	\label{eq:component-energy}
	\frac{E_\mathrm{had}}{E_0} = \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{n_\mathrm{H}},
	\qquad
	\frac{E_\mathrm{EM}}{E_0} = 1 -\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^{n_\mathrm{H}}.
\end{equation}

Combining both insights and approximating the number of particles in the shower
at the maximum as $N_i = E_i\,/\,E^\mathrm{crit.}_i$, it becomes evident that
the hadronic fraction of air showers increases with $A$. This directly 
translates to a higher muon content for primaries at the same energy, which can
be verified in \cref{fig:eas-simulations}.

\subsubsection{Photon Showers}
\label{sssec:photon-showers}

This picture reverses for photon primaries. Only few hadrons and muons are 
present in the resulting air showers (Ref.\ \cref{fig:eas-photon}). However,
conclusions on the nature of the primary particle based on an observed muon
count are often flawed. 

Low energy, light hadrons can have very low muon counts due to 
shower-to-shower fluctuations. This makes them appear very similar to 
photon-initiated \EASs. \cref{fig:mu-primaries} shows the distribution of 
simulated muon densities for proton and photon showers at different sampling 
points. While a clear difference between the distributions can be seen for the 
two primaries, a considerable overlap exists. This makes a discrimination on 
the basis of a single observation very challenging. 

The discrimination of photons and hadrons is thus usually conducted in a 
probabilistic context. Instead of a single observation, the muon content must
be sampled at many distances from the primary axis for a given air shower 
event, and many air shower events must be analyzed for a complete picture
\cite{gonzalezSearchesPrimaryPhotons2025}, as done in \todo{discrimination 
chapter}.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.34\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/muon_ldf.png}
  \label{fig:mu-primaries}
  }\hspace{0.2cm}
  \subfloat[]{\includegraphics[height=0.34\textwidth]{cosmic-rays/ldf_beta.png}
  \label{fig:beta-ldf}
  }
  \caption[]{\subref{fig:mu-primaries} The reconstructed density of 
  muons for simulated air showers of different energies for protons 
  (\textit{top}), and photons (\textit{bottom}). Muons appear far less 
  frequently for \EM showers, and are usually concentrated more closely to the 
  shower axis. \subref{fig:beta-ldf} The influence of the $\beta$ shape 
  parameters on the \acs{LDF} form. The value of the \acs{LDF} at the reference
  length $r_\mathrm{ref.} = \SI{1000}{\meter}$ is marked with dash-dotted 
  lines.}
  \label{fig:muon-ldf}
\end{figure}

\todo{redo plots with more data}

\subsection{Detection Channels}
\label{ssec:detection-channels}

The final pages in this chapter are dedicated to listing the observables by 
which modern \CR observatories detect cosmic rays. With this work in particular
focusing on \CRs with energies surpassing \SI{100}{\peta\eV}, the focus lies on
the indirect detection via observations of \EASs. See for reference also
\cref{chap:pierre-auger-observatory}.

\subsubsection{Particle Footprint}
\label{sssec:particle-footprint}

The shower maximum of \EASs with energies above $\sim\SI{100}{\tera\eV}$ occurs
sufficiently deep in the atmosphere such that secondary particles reach the
ground before thermalizing \cite{schoorlemmerCharacteristicsExtensiveAir2019}. 
\cref{fig:eas-simulations} in particular shows the distribution of particles at
sea level for a variation of primary particles with an energy of 
\SI{100}{\tera\eV}.

Like many air shower characteristics it is impossible to describe the 
distribution of particles on the ground exactly. Instead, empirical or 
theoretical models can be employed to approximate the number of particles (of a
certain type) that are expected for a given air shower event. These models are 
called \LDFs. One well known parametrization for the muon \LDF is given by the 
\NKG equation \cite{kamataLateralAngularStructure1958, 
greisenCosmicRayShowers1960}

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:NKG}
S(r) = S(r_\mathrm{ref.})\,\left( \frac{r}{r_\mathrm{ref.}}  \right)^\beta\,\left( \frac{r + r_s}{r_\mathrm{ref.} + r_s}  \right)^{\beta + \gamma}.
\end{equation}

In \cref{eq:NKG}, $r_\mathrm{ref.}$ gives a constant reference length, 
typically defined by detector characteristics, whereas $\beta$ and $\gamma$ are
the shape parameters of the \LDF that determine the \LDF form. They are
parametrized by the \EAS characteristics such as the zenith angle, energy or 
shower age \cite{jesusStudyMassComposition2025}. The impact on the \LDF form 
that the choice of $\beta$ has is visualized in \cref{fig:beta-ldf}. 

\subsubsection{Fluorescence Light}
\label{sssec:fluorescence-light}

In the theoretical treatment of the shower in \cref{ssec:cascade-model} it is 
assumed that ionization does not heavily influence the cascade development up 
to the shower maximum. In fact, typical ionization losses for particles with 
energies exceeding \SI{1}{\giga\eV} are just \SI{3}{\percent}. Closer to the 
shower maximum where the average energy per particles is lower this fraction 
increases tenfold \cite{risseEnergyReleaseAir2004}. Integrating along the 
entire shower axis, this corresponds very rougly to \SIrange{50}{70}{\percent} 
of the primary energy eventually being absorbed by molecules in the atmosphere.

A fraction of this energy is reemitted in the form of light. Molecular 
excitations of (mostly) N$_2$ are formed from the relativistic interactions,
and decay back to the ground state by emission of a \SI{337.1}{\nano\meter} 
photon \cite{leanContributionUltravioletIrradiance1989, 
aveSpectrallyResolvedPressure2008}. The \UV luminosity of the \EAS is 
determined by the fluorescence yield, $Y_\mathrm{Air}$, which gives the number 
of photons created per deposited unit of energy 
\cite{keilhauerNitrogenFluorescenceAir2013}. The higher the fluorescence yield,
the brighter the shower appears in the \UV band. Independent of
$Y_\mathrm{Air}$, the luminosity also increases the more energy is deposited,
which is of course dependant on the shower age. As such, the fluorescence 
signal can be used to study the longitudinal profile of \EASs. An example
observation of the fluorescence light emitted by an air shower is shown in
\cref{fig:fd-profile} on page \pageref{fig:fd-profile}.

\subsubsection{Cherenkov Light}
\label{sssec:cherenkov-light}

The fluorescence light is not the only component that comprises the total \UV 
signal of an \EAS. The secondary particles in the shower front are
ultrarelativistic, and as such produce Cherenkov light. Unlike the fluorescence
emission that is emitted isotropically, the Cherenkov radiation has a 
(comparably) high brilliance. The opening angle of the emitted \UV light cone, 
$\cos\theta_\mathrm{C} = 1 / n\beta$, is very shallow. Typically the footprint 
of the Cherenkov light is contained within \SI{100}{\meter}, slightly more than
one Molière radius, $r_0$ \cite{nerlingDescriptionCherenkovLight2005, 
pierreaugercollaborationAtmosphericEffectsExtensive2009}. This compactness 
allows for the efficient observation of \EASs with earthbound \UV telescopes as
detailed in e.g.\ \cite{baixerasMAGICTelescope2003}.

\subsubsection{Radio Emission}
\label{sssec:radio-emission}

Finally, the interplay between the particle cascade and the geomagnetic field 
creates a radio wavelength signature by which \EASs can be detected. A given
(charged) particle in the shower front experiences Lorentz deflection from 
traversing the geomagnetic field, and emits faint Bremsstrahlung as a 
consequence. On top of this, \EASs typically feature an excess of electrons 
(over positrons) by $\sim\SI{10}{\percent}$
\cite{askaryanExcessNegativeCharge1961}, in part due to the annihilation of 
positrons in the dielectric medium. Due to this charge excess the emissions of 
Cherenkov light, transition radiation, and Bremsstrahlung are altered according
to the Askaryan effect \cite{askaryanExcessNegativeCharge1961}. This resonantly
boosts the \EM signature for all charged particles in the shower front.

The total radiation signal is given by the sum of contributions of all particle
in the shower. These can (given the right circumstances) constructively 
interfere with one another to yield strong electrical field strenghts, 
characterized in \cite{horandelRadioDetectionExtensive2019, 
allanProgressElementaryParticle1971} as

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:radio-ldf}
|\vec{E}|(r) = \frac{10}{11} \left(\frac{E}{\SI{100}{\tera\eV}}\right)\left(\frac{\sin\alpha\cos\theta}{\sin\,\SI{45}{\degree}\cos\,\SI{30}{\degree}} \right)\exp\left(\frac{r}{\SI{110}{\meter}}\right)\,\unit{\upmu\volt\per\meter\per\mega\hertz}.
\end{equation}

The strong coherence criteria restrict the emission to the 
\SIrange{30}{80}{\mega\hertz} frequency band, and close to the Cherenkov cone
of the shower (Ref.\ \cref{sssec:cherenkov-light}).
